<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eugène de Beauharnais</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1781–1824</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Prince Eugène</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eugène de Beauharnais was the son of Joséphine and the adopted stepson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> he governed the Napoleonic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> between 1805 and 1814 and commanded armies in the campaigns of 1809 and 1812. In 1810 he was named heir to the Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, and after Napoleon's fall his father-in-law, the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, made him Duke of Leuchtenberg in 1817.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eugene-de-beauharnais.jpg" alt="Eugène de Beauharnais" width="320" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrea Appiani · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Prince_Eugene_de_Beauharnais,_Viceroy_of_Italy_and_Duke_of_Leuchtenberg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
